  <h1>About Doodle Heroes</h1>
  <p class="lede">A small, independent site of logic puzzles set on a hand-drawn island. The aim is simple: puzzles that are fair, that teach you something, and that you can play anywhere without signing up.</p>

  <h2>Fair puzzles only</h2>
  <p>The worst moment in a logic puzzle is realising the only way forward is to guess. We don’t publish puzzles like that. Every puzzle is run through a <strong>deduction solver</strong> that is only allowed the reasoning a person can do — one row, one island or one tile at a time, using the techniques in our guides. If it can’t finish the puzzle, the puzzle is redrawn or replaced. Because every step it takes is forced, a puzzle it finishes can only have one solution.</p>
  <p>For Isle Spans and Lighthouse Wiring we also run a second, independent check: a <strong>full search</strong> that ignores technique entirely and confirms there is exactly one solution.</p>
  <p>Island Sketch pictures are drawn by hand. Isle Spans maps and Lighthouse Wiring grids are grown by our own generators from fixed seeds, then picked by hand so each chapter gets steadily harder. Daily puzzles are generated from the date and pass the same solver before they’re shown. That’s also what makes the hints useful: when you ask for one, the game shows you a real deduction you could have made, not a random square.</p>

  <h2>Teaching, not just playing</h2>
  <p>We think logic puzzles get better as you understand them, so a lot of this site is written explanation: <a href="/island-sketch#rules">the rules with examples</a>, <a href="/guides">guides to the techniques</a>, and <a href="/walkthroughs/the-lighthouse">walkthroughs that solve a whole puzzle</a> one deduction at a time. Every example board in those pages is generated from the same solver as the game, so the diagrams can’t drift out of step with the rules.</p>

  <h2>What’s here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/island-sketch">Island Sketch</a> — picture logic: fill squares from number clues to reveal a hand-drawn sketch.</li>
    <li><a href="/isle-spans">Isle Spans</a> — bridge building: join every island with bridges matching its number.</li>
    <li><a href="/lighthouse-wiring">Lighthouse Wiring</a> — rotating tiles: power every cottage from the lighthouse.</li>
    <li><a href="/expedition">Lantern Isles</a> — an expedition that links all three into four chapters of a story.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each game has twelve puzzles in three chapters, a daily challenge, and its own rules page, guides and walkthrough.</p>

  <h2>How the site works</h2>
  <p>Doodle Heroes is a static website. There is no account system and no server-side database. Your progress — boards in progress, stars and best times — is saved in your own browser and never sent to us; you can see or clear it on the <a href="/progress">progress page</a>.</p>
  <p>Pages with written content carry advertising from Google AdSense, which pays for the domain and hosting. The puzzle screens themselves never show ads, so there’s nothing to mis-tap while you play. See the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for details.</p>

  <h2>Credits</h2>
  <p>All puzzles, pictures, guides and code on this site are original work made for Doodle Heroes. The three puzzle types are traditional logic-puzzle forms whose rules are shared by many publishers: picture logic (also known as nonograms), bridge puzzles (also known as Hashiwokakero or Hashi) and rotating network puzzles. Our puzzles, presentation, generators and solvers are our own. Text is set in Fredoka and Caveat from Google Fonts, used under the SIL Open Font License.</p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>Found a bug, a mistake in a guide, or a puzzle that seems to need a guess? We’d like to know — see the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</p>
